<http://example.org/story/shepherd_aga_daughter> a schema:TextObject ;
    dcterms:title "Shepherd, Aga, and Daughter Story" ;
    schema:description """Once upon a time there was a shepherd. He loved the daughter of a rich, respected man, an 'aga, who did not want to give him the girl as his wife, but also did not want to turn him coldly away. So he set up a condition, which he assumed, the boy would not be able to fulfill. The shepherd should lock up his sheep for a week, and should only let them lick salt, but should not lead them to water. And then, when he had let the herd free, he should succeed in preventing the sheep from running to the water, despite their great thirst. The young shepherd agreed to this condition, and when a week was up, he played such a lovely melody on his flute that the sheep stayed with him and listened. He had passed the test and received the daughter of the aga as his wife.""" ;
    ns2:hasSymbolism <http://example.org/symbolism/magical_power_of_music> .

<http://example.org/symbolism/magical_power_of_music> a ns2:CACAO_0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "Magical Power of Music" ;
    schema:description "A last rest of the original belief in the magical power of music, as the shepherds and the nomads (in their connectedness to nature) once possessed." ;
